---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Analogy from '../../components/Analogy.astro';
import MaterialChart from '../../components/MaterialChart.astro';
import { MATERIALS } from '../../data/materials.ts';

// Heat diagram: where common hot places sit against each plastic's softening point.
const T0 = 20, T1 = 110;
const X = (t: number) => 40 + ((t - T0) / (T1 - T0)) * 520;
const places = [
  { t: 37, label: 'In your hand' },
  { t: 65, label: 'Dishwasher' },
  { t: 75, label: 'Car dashboard, summer' },
];
const mats = MATERIALS.filter((m) => m.heatC && m.id !== 'silk');
---

<Guide slug="materials">
  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>
    Most "my print broke" stories aren't bad printing. The right object was printed in the wrong plastic. A PLA phone
    mount left on a car dashboard in July will sag into a sad noodle. A business card in TPU will flop. The material
    decides where your print can live.
  </p>

  <h2>The intuition</h2>
  <Analogy title="Chocolate, toffee and gummy bears">
    <p>
      <strong>PLA</strong> is like a chocolate bar: crisp, holds sharp detail, snaps if you bend it hard, and goes soft
      in a hot car. <strong>PETG</strong> is more like toffee: a bit tougher, bends before it breaks, and copes with
      more heat. <strong>TPU</strong> is a gummy bear: you can squash it and it springs back, but it won't hold a
      shape under load.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where it breaks down: unlike chocolate, PLA doesn't melt into a puddle at body temperature. It only softens well
      above it, at around 55 °C.
    </p>
  </Analogy>

  <p>The quickest way to choose is heat. Find where the part will live on this scale and stay to the left of its plastic:</p>

  <figure class="panel heat">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 600 190" role="img" aria-label="Softening temperatures of plastics compared with hot places">
      <desc>
        A temperature scale from 20 to 110 degrees Celsius. Hot places (hand 37 °C, dishwasher about 65 °C, car
        dashboard about 75 °C) are marked above it; the typical softening temperatures of PLA (55 °C), PETG (70 °C) and
        ASA (93 °C) are marked below it.
      </desc>
      <defs>
        <linearGradient id="heatgrad" x1="0" x2="1">
          <stop offset="0" stop-color="#3a6ea5"></stop>
          <stop offset="0.5" stop-color="#f2c14e"></stop>
          <stop offset="1" stop-color="#ff4d2e"></stop>
        </linearGradient>
      </defs>
      <rect x={X(T0)} y="88" width={X(T1) - X(T0)} height="10" rx="5" fill="url(#heatgrad)"></rect>
      {
        [20, 40, 60, 80, 100].map((t) => (
          <text x={X(t)} y="114" text-anchor="middle" class="tick">
            {t} °C
          </text>
        ))
      }
      {
        places.map((p, i) => (
          <g>
            <line x1={X(p.t)} x2={X(p.t)} y1={30 + i * 18} y2="86" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
            <text x={X(p.t) + 6} y={34 + i * 18} class="place">
              {p.label} ≈{p.t} °C
            </text>
          </g>
        ))
      }
      {
        mats.map((m, i) => (
          <g>
            <line x1={X(m.heatC!)} x2={X(m.heatC!)} y1="100" y2={136 + i * 16} stroke="var(--accent)" />
            <circle cx={X(m.heatC!)} cy="93" r="6" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" />
            <text x={X(m.heatC!) + 6} y={140 + i * 16} class="mat">
              {m.name} softens ≈{m.heatC} °C
            </text>
          </g>
        ))
      }
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="hint">Typical values. Car interiors vary a lot with sun, colour and climate. Not to scale vertically.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Compare them</h2>
  <p>
    The ratings are my judgement from printing with each one, relative to each other. They're not lab measurements. The
    temperatures and densities are typical manufacturer data-sheet values.
  </p>
  <div class="wide"><MaterialChart /></div>

    <h2>Worked example: a phone stand for the car</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Where will it live?</strong> On a dashboard, which in summer can sit around 75 °C.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the heat scale.</strong> PLA (≈55 °C) and PETG (≈70 °C) are both below that, so either could sag. ASA (≈93 °C) is comfortably above.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the other needs.</strong> It gets knocked about (toughness: ASA 4/5) and sits in sunlight (outdoor/UV: ASA 5/5).</li>
      <li><strong>Choose ASA.</strong> The same stand on a desk at home? PLA is fine, cheaper and crisper.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Where this stops</h2>
    <div class="stops">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Not food-safe by default.</strong> Even "food-safe" filament has tiny gaps between layers where bacteria live. Treat prints as decorative unless agreed otherwise.</li>
        <li><strong>Softening isn't one temperature.</strong> A part under load creeps at lower temperatures than an unloaded one. The numbers above are for short exposure.</li>
        <li><strong>Brands differ.</strong> "PLA+" and blends vary from these typical values. Ask if your part has to meet a real requirement.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>References</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Prusament PLA, PETG and ASA technical data sheets (Prusa Research): density and heat deflection temperature.</li>
      <li>Bambu Lab PLA Basic, PETG HF and TPU 95A technical data sheets.</li>
    </ul>
</Guide>

<style>
  .heat {
    margin: 20px 0;
    padding: 16px;
  }
  .heat svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .tick {
    font: 500 10px var(--mono);
    fill: var(--text-3);
  }
  .place {
    font: 500 11px var(--font);
    fill: var(--text-2);
  }
  .mat {
    font: 600 11px var(--font);
    fill: var(--accent);
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
</style>
